-The first physical torque measurements of the redesigned extensor route have been collected. In the first test of the $\diameter$\qty{20}{\mm} extensor on the rebuilt route, three isometric points were held near full extension: measured knee torque of \qty{1.163}{\newton\cdot\meter} at a knee angle of \ang{10}, \qty{7.849}{\newton\cdot\meter} at \ang{11}, and \qty{23.437}{\newton\cdot\meter} at \ang{10}, against hung loads of \qtylist{3.7;25.2;76}{\newton} at gauge pressures of \qtylist{388.5;388;592}{\kPa}, respectively. The first and third points sit at essentially the same knee angle and span a twentyfold range of held torque, so they begin to map the route's response near extension as pressure and load vary. These three points are the start of the torque-versus-angle validation of the redesigned route against its required envelope; the full sweep, and the corresponding flexor measurements, are pending.
+Physical testing of the redesigned extensor route has begun on the test stand with the $\diameter$\qty{20}{\mm} actuator. In the first test, on the earlier direct route, three isometric points were held near full extension: measured knee torque of \qty{1.163}{\newton\meter} at a knee angle of \ang{10}, \qty{7.849}{\newton\meter} at \ang{11}, and \qty{23.437}{\newton\meter} at \ang{10}, against hung loads of \qtylist{3.7;25.2;76}{\newton} at gauge pressures of \qtylist{388.5;388;592}{\kPa}, respectively. The first and third points sit at essentially the same knee angle and span a twentyfold range of held torque, so they begin to map the route's response near extension as pressure and load vary. On the rebuilt cam route, a nine-point isometric sweep then held loads of \qtylist{100;116;126;140;168;173.4;180.2;181;181.5}{\newton} at knee angles from \ang{-119} to \ang{5} and gauge pressures from \qty{256}{\kPa} to \qty{488}{\kPa}, with the load-cell angle of every hold recorded in the tibia frame and each measured wrench referred to the moving knee center through the adjoint transform already used for the identification data, at the measured reaction-point distance of \qty{259.9}{\mm}. The adjoint-reduced torques are entered for the six most recent holds and span \qty{28.8}{\newton\meter} to \qty{50.6}{\newton\meter}, with the reduction of the three earliest holds in progress. The pressures read against the design on their own: the deep-flexion hold at \ang{-119} was carried at \qty{256}{\kPa}, just below the low end of the cam design's predicted \qtyrange{269}{507}{\kPa} requirement band, and the extension holds at \ang{-1} and \ang{5} at \qty{488}{\kPa} and \qty{480}{\kPa} against the \qty{507}{\kPa} worst case, and the route carried every hold of the sweep without nearing the \qty{620}{\kPa} ceiling. The measured-versus-predicted envelope comparison for the sweep, and the corresponding flexor measurements, are pending.
